    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by two high-upside plays that clear the 70-point threshold: **Junior Caminero** (79.84) and **Yordan Alvarez** (72.94). **Junior Caminero** benefits immensely from a soft matchup against Jacob Lopez, whose 5.06 xERA is the most exploitable on the board today. When paired with favorable park factors and the +19% boost from the SP skill gap, **Junior Caminero** is our primary source of power. Meanwhile, **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in start; despite a neutral park, his elite xwOBA of 0.467 and favorable wind conditions create a massive ceiling. While we continue to monitor the status of **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Colt Keith**, and **Dillon Dingler**, all of whom are sidelined today, the depth provided by **Trea Turner** and **Caleb Durbin**\u2014both scoring well into the 60s\u2014ensures our stolen base potential remains high despite the scarcity of the category.\n\nStrategic decisions for the bottom of the order heavily favored platoon efficiency. **Christian Yelich** earned his spot with a 58.30 score, driven by a +13% dynamic platoon boost against Casey Mize, effectively pushing him ahead of **Nelson Velazquez** (52.86). While **Nelson Velazquez** was the closest bench consideration, the matchup against Andrew Painter (4.55 xERA) wasn't quite potent enough to overcome the platoon advantages held by **Christian Yelich** and **Lars Nootbaar**. Risks today are localized: we are closely tracking the 31% rain risk for **Carson Benge** in his tilt against Bryce Elder, though his 61.23 score makes him a must-start if the weather holds. Elsewhere, we are proceeding with caution regarding the pending lineups for **Gary Sanchez** and **Josh Smith**, as any move lower in the order for them could compress their efficiency scores further. As we continue to develop prospects like **Chase Meidroth**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez**, the core stays focused on maximizing daily efficiency to maintain our Roto standing.",
